---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyArea from '@/components/tools/everyday/CalcMoneyArea.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="flooring-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyArea client:load variant="flooring" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Rooms in, boxes out</h2>
    <p>
      Flooring is sold by the carton, and each product covers a different area per carton. The calculation
      always has three steps:
    </p>
    <pre><code>area           = Σ (length × width) for every room
area to buy    = area × (1 + waste)
boxes          = area to buy ÷ coverage per box, rounded up</code></pre>
    <p>
      A 300 square foot job at 10% waste needs 330 square feet. At 20.1 sq ft per carton (a very common
      laminate figure), that is 17 boxes, which cover 341.7 sq ft. You must round up, because you cannot buy
      two-thirds of a carton.
    </p>

    <h2>Coverage per box varies enormously</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[440px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Product</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Typical sq ft / box</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Typical m² / box</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>Laminate</td><td style="text-align:right">18–24</td><td style="text-align:right">1.7–2.2</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Luxury vinyl plank (LVP)</td><td style="text-align:right">24–36</td><td style="text-align:right">2.2–3.3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Engineered hardwood</td><td style="text-align:right">20–30</td><td style="text-align:right">1.9–2.8</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Solid hardwood</td><td style="text-align:right">18–24</td><td style="text-align:right">1.7–2.2</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Floor tile (12 × 12 in)</td><td style="text-align:right">15–16</td><td style="text-align:right">1.4–1.5</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm">
      Always read the end label. Most cartons give the coverage in square feet and square metres, and it
      changes between ranges from the same manufacturer.
    </p>

    <h2>How much waste</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>7–10%: a simple rectangular room, planks along the long side.</li>
      <li>10–15%: several rooms, doorways, a hallway, or an irregular plan.</li>
      <li>15–20%: diagonal or herringbone layouts, which give many angled off-cuts.</li>
      <li>+ 1 box: keep it sealed for future repairs, because ranges are often discontinued.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Long narrow rooms waste more than square rooms of the same area, because each row has more end cuts
      and fewer chances to start with an off-cut from the previous row.
    </p>

    <h2>Measure to the wall, then add the expansion gap</h2>
    <p>
      Floating floors (laminate and most LVP) need an expansion gap of 8–12 mm (about 3/8 in) around the
      whole perimeter, hidden under skirting or beading. Measure from wall face to wall face and let the
      waste allowance include the gap. Do not subtract it, because you must still buy and cut the material.
    </p>
    <p>
      Include doorways and thresholds in the measurement if the flooring goes through them. The flooring
      also continues under the skirting or beading, so the visible floor is slightly smaller than the
      quantity you buy.
    </p>

    <h2>Which direction should the planks run?</h2>
    <p>
      The convention is to lay planks parallel to the longest wall, or towards the main light source,
      because the room then looks longer and the seams are less visible. On a suspended timber subfloor,
      planks must be perpendicular to the joists for stability, which can override the appearance. The
      direction does not change the quantity much, but a diagonal layout adds about five percentage points
      of waste.
    </p>

    <h2>What else to order</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Underlay: usually sold in rolls of 100–200 sq ft. Some laminates have it attached; check so you do
        not buy it twice.
      </li>
      <li>
        Moisture barrier: required over concrete slabs and in basements, sometimes part of the underlay.
      </li>
      <li>
        Transition strips: one per doorway, plus one at each change of material.
      </li>
      <li>
        Beading or new skirting: measured by the perimeter in linear feet or metres, not by area.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      To order underlay room by room, add a separate row per room and read the area of each room in the
      table.
    </p>

    <h2>Acclimatisation, before you cut anything</h2>
    <p>
      Wood and laminate move with humidity. Most manufacturers require the boxes to lie flat in the
      installation room for 48–72 hours before installation, and refuse a warranty claim if they did not.
      Order early enough for this.
    </p>

    <h2>Batch numbers</h2>
    <p>
      As with tile, flooring is made in batches, and the shade varies between them. Buy the whole job,
      including waste, in one order, and check that the batch code is the same on all cartons before you
      start. It is much easier to exchange a box at the merchant than to remove a finished row.
    </p>

    <h2>Runs offline, uploads nothing</h2>
    <p>
      The flooring calculator, the box count and the CSV, Excel and PDF exports are JavaScript in your
      browser. The page works in an empty house with no signal, and your room list stays on the device
      unless you export it.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
